---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import KickerLabel from '../components/KickerLabel.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Thesis"
  description="Five Necessities, four markets, the T1 / T2 filter. The concise version of The Necessity Doctrine."
>
  <article class="thesis container">
    <header class="thesis__head">
      <KickerLabel index="00" label="The thesis · concise" />
      <h1 class="thesis__title">Necessity is the thesis.</h1>
      <p class="thesis__lede">
        Seed-stage capital deployed where regulation defines the floor of operation and the compliance layer has not yet been built. Four markets. Five Necessities. Twelve minutes.
      </p>
      <nav class="thesis__toc" aria-label="Sections">
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#inevitability">1. The inevitability</a></li>
          <li><a href="#five">2. The Five Necessities</a></li>
          <li><a href="#filter">3. The T1 / T2 filter</a></li>
          <li><a href="#markets">4. The four markets</a></li>
          <li><a href="#window">5. The Crystallisation Window</a></li>
          <li><a href="#certainties">6. Five structural certainties</a></li>
          <li><a href="#doctrine">7. Read the full doctrine</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section id="inevitability" class="thesis__section">
      <h2>1. The inevitability</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Food contamination. Missed diagnoses. Grid failure. Autonomous-system error. Compromised supply chains. Civilisational necessities where society has already decided that market forces alone are insufficient, governed precisely because demand in them cannot be allowed to fail.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is how the fund knows, with higher confidence than any other signal available at seed, that demand will persist. The trigger is the moment a buyer, operator, or manufacturer cannot legally launch, connect, sell, export, insure, claim subsidy eligibility, or continue operating without machine-readable proof.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="five" class="thesis__section">
      <h2>2. The Five Necessities</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Five sectors meet the test across the four target markets. Each carries non-discretionary demand, a binding regulatory or statutory trigger, and a compliance layer that has not been built. Regulation defines the floor, not the feature, and the company that owns the proof layer becomes the default integration point for every operator that has to clear it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="necs">
        <li>
          <span class="necs__num">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Food Security Systems</h3>
            <p>Food control plans, verifier workflows, traceability, residue testing, recall readiness, import and export assurance, biosecurity.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="necs__num">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Biomedical Systems</h3>
            <p>Clinical software, diagnostics, digital therapeutics, AI-assisted decision support, software-linked medical devices.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="necs__num">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Power Systems</h3>
            <p>Grid orchestration, storage, demand response, EV charging, distributed energy, building-grid interaction.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="necs__num">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Autonomous Systems</h3>
            <p>Autonomous road, logistics, public-path, maritime, aviation, and dual-use systems.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="necs__num">05</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Critical Materials and Supply Integrity</h3>
            <p>Provenance, chain of custody, processing history, ESG evidence, buyer eligibility for strategic minerals and battery inputs.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="thesis__note kicker">Data and AI Governance and Financial Integrity sit across all five as horizontal infrastructure, not as standalone Necessities.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="filter" class="thesis__section">
      <h2>3. The T1 / T2 filter</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The framework is binary. Anything more elaborate collapses under the weight of subjective scoring.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Tier 1. Licence-as-barrier.</strong> The company cannot legally operate without regulatory approval: a licence, a clearance, a certification. The barrier is permission to operate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Tier 2. Compulsion-as-barrier.</strong> The buyer is statutorily required to purchase compliant solutions: a procurement obligation, a compliance mandate, a reporting requirement. The barrier is evidence of compliance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Applied to the four-market universe of 19,416 classified technology companies, the screen produces 6,505 in-thesis companies, a 33.5% conservative-classifier share. Adding the seed-stage filter narrows the addressable universe to 2,625.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="markets" class="thesis__section">
      <h2>4. The four markets</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Australia, Japan, New Zealand, Singapore. Chosen because they are the four developed APAC markets where the regulation map produces a reliable result: public-source regulatory calendars, traceable parliamentary records, and an exit lane to NA and EMEA strategics that is documented and active.
        </p>
        <p>
          The four markets contain 174 of 725 mapped regulations globally and 117 Tier 1 regulations across the Five Necessities. Allocation: Australia 35%, Japan 30%, New Zealand 20%, Singapore 15%.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="window" class="thesis__section">
      <h2>5. The Crystallisation Window</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The gap between a regulation's effective date and the moment a compliant operational layer exists. Open now across all four target markets and closing as platforms form between 2025 and 2030.
        </p>
        <p>
          36 in-flight regulations dated 2024 to 2030 concentrate in 2026 to 2028 inside the fund's four-year investment period: AU 13, JP 11, SG 10, NZ 2. The fund does not need to predict which companies fill the categories. It needs to deploy before the categories are filled.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="certainties" class="thesis__section">
      <h2>6. Five structural certainties</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Each is observable today in the four-market dataset, not a forecast.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="certs">
        <li><strong>Seed entry is at near-parity. Exit is at premium.</strong> T1 round-size premium runs 1.12x at seed and 1.53x at Series C. Regulated exit-rate multiple lands at 2.22x unregulated peers over the 10-to-15-year window.</li>
        <li><strong>Regulation is the sieve for defensible returns at every stage.</strong> 67.9% of the 19,416-company four-market universe sits behind a T1 or T2 barrier.</li>
        <li><strong>Regulation absorbs capital at an accelerating rate.</strong> By Series C, 82.0% of the four-market universe's funding flows to regulated companies.</li>
        <li><strong>A global strategic-acquirer buyer set exists and is active.</strong> Nine recent named developed-APAC regulated-company acquisitions, 2020 to 2026, primary-source verified.</li>
        <li><strong>Developed APAC sets frontier regulation in the Necessity categories the fund underwrites.</strong> 36 in-flight regulations dated 2024 to 2030, concentrated in the fund's investment window.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="doctrine" class="thesis__section thesis__cta">
      <h2>7. Read the full doctrine</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The Necessity Doctrine is the long-form artefact. Ten chapters, the Crystallisation Window in detail, the four-market profiles, and the two branching paths the next decade splits into. Read it in full or download the PDF.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="thesis__cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/doctrine/">Read the doctrine</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/doctrine/download/">Download the PDF</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .thesis { padding-block: var(--s-8) var(--s-9); max-width: 56rem; }
  .thesis__head { margin-block-end: var(--s-8); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--s-4); }
  .thesis__title {
    font-family: var(--f-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-3xl);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .thesis__lede {
    font-family: var(--f-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: var(--fs-xl);
    line-height: var(--lh-snug);
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    max-width: 52ch;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .thesis__toc {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-5);
    padding: var(--s-5);
    background: var(--bg-warm);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--c-tomato);
  }
  .thesis__toc ol {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--s-2) var(--s-5);
    font-family: var(--f-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
  }
  .thesis__toc a {
    text-decoration: none;
    color: var(--fg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
  }
  .thesis__toc a:hover {
    color: var(--c-tomato);
    border-bottom-color: currentColor;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .thesis__toc ol { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .thesis__section {
    padding-block: var(--s-7);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    scroll-margin-top: var(--s-7);
  }
  .thesis__section:last-of-type { border-bottom: 0; }
  .thesis__section h2 {
    font-family: var(--f-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-2xl);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-5);
  }

  .necs {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--s-5) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-5);
  }
  .necs li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-block-end: var(--s-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .necs li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .necs__num {
    font-family: var(--f-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    color: var(--c-tomato);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }
  .necs h3 {
    font-family: var(--f-display);
    font-size: var(--fs-lg);
    margin: 0 0 var(--s-2);
  }
  .necs p { margin: 0; font-size: var(--fs-sm); color: var(--fg-soft); line-height: 1.55; }

  .certs {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    counter-reset: cert;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--s-3);
  }
  .certs li {
    counter-increment: cert;
    position: relative;
    padding-inline-start: var(--s-6);
    font-size: var(--fs-md);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
  }
  .certs li::before {
    content: counter(cert, decimal-leading-zero);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.1em;
    font-family: var(--f-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-xs);
    color: var(--c-tomato);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  }

  .thesis__note {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-4);
    color: var(--fg-soft);
  }

  .thesis__cta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-block-start: var(--s-5);
  }
</style>
